WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:02.730
- I can't choose any of you
guys to be the mole, like...

00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:04.265
- This is gonna be the hardest thing.

00:00:04.265 --> 00:00:05.400
- Like even me, myself, like, it's like,

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:07.686
I think I'm the mole, honestly.

00:00:07.686 --> 00:00:10.769
(calm hip-hop music)

00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:21.650
- So I think we just talk
about our disabilities.

00:00:21.650 --> 00:00:23.400
- I, I prefer disorder.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:24.900
- Disorder, yeah.

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:27.900
I was diagnosed in middle school

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:32.040
and then I transitioned to a school

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:34.410
for people on the spectrum.

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:36.870
Once I graduated middle
school and went to that school

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:40.680
I kind of really enjoyed
where I was at and everything.

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:42.120
This is really hard 'cause there's like

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:44.130
so many eyes on me already.

00:00:44.130 --> 00:00:46.470
It's just, it's just
taken a while to accept

00:00:46.470 --> 00:00:48.180
that I have a disorder.

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:51.787
There's people who I've
met myself who say,

00:00:51.787 --> 00:00:52.830
"you have autism".

00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:53.797
And my family would be like,

00:00:53.797 --> 00:00:55.260
"did you tell 'em you have an...

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:56.760
did you tell 'em you have a disability?"

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:58.470
No, I don't think I need to.

00:00:58.470 --> 00:01:01.500
But reality, it really does help to know

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:03.030
that I do have autism.

00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:04.020
- They first suspected me

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:06.540
of being autistic when I was five,

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:10.350
but they, they were, they
hesitated to diagnose me

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:12.060
because I was too intelligent.

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:13.800
- I mean that's a compliment right there.

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:15.507
Being too intelligent.

00:01:15.507 --> 00:01:17.820
- I feel like in some ways it
was a little bit of an insult.

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:20.100
- I was diagnosed at the age of two.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:23.520
I don't see it as a disorder
or a disability because

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:25.350
I don't see my autism as something

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:28.053
that's negatively affected my life.

00:01:28.053 --> 00:01:30.240
'Cause like, I like who I
am, I like the way I think

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:32.550
and I don't want to change
a single thing about myself.

00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:34.770
- Honestly. Like being
here is like we have to...

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:36.450
Oh, go ahead Amanda.
Sorry. I've spoken so much.

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:38.850
- I personally see autism as a disability.

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:40.860
I have a master's in disability studies.

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:43.080
The medical model of
disability inherently says

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:46.620
that autism and any other
disability is within our own fault

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:48.900
and that we are the
cause of the disability.

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:51.150
But the social model of disability says

00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:53.850
that society is the
cause of the disability.

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:55.380
So autism is a disability

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:57.540
because society is not made for me.

00:01:57.540 --> 00:01:59.970
That disability comes
from without, not within.

00:01:59.970 --> 00:02:03.420
I grew up autistic and
deaf and I grew up feeling

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:06.270
like I was such an
outcast and, and you know,

00:02:06.270 --> 00:02:08.160
and I was the odd one out.

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:10.910
Oh hey, I just used the name
of the show! That was fun.

00:02:12.557 --> 00:02:15.030
- I was diagnosed when
I was four years old

00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:17.190
and um, it's um, you know, like it's,

00:02:17.190 --> 00:02:18.900
it's always been a
difficult process for me.

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:20.670
You know, like basically
growing up, you know,

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:22.500
going to school and everything.

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:26.100
You know that people don't
perceive me as this normal guy.

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:29.340
So I figure, I figure if I'm
gonna be this autistic person

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:30.930
you know, like might as well be

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:32.850
the best autistic person that I can be.

00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:33.750
You know, I always...

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:34.583
- Preach!

00:02:34.583 --> 00:02:37.140
- Yeah, I mean I always try
my best to excel in school.

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:39.300
You know, I always tend to
be like one of the smartest,

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:40.740
smartest kid in school, you know.

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:42.030
You know that's a whole stereotype.

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:43.710
- I would consider myself a late bloomer.

00:02:43.710 --> 00:02:45.990
I was diagnosed at the age of 22.

00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:48.480
I went most of my life always knowing

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:51.750
that there was, there's
something different about me,

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:53.310
but I couldn't put my finger on it.

00:02:53.310 --> 00:02:56.520
The reason why I only
got diagnosed this year

00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:58.350
was because I recently moved

00:02:58.350 --> 00:03:01.830
to a large metropolitan
area that has many resources

00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:05.130
and so that was one of the best decisions

00:03:05.130 --> 00:03:06.390
I've ever made in my life

00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:09.450
because I have access to therapy

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:11.220
and I was finally able to get an answer

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:12.720
that I was always looking for.

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:15.540
I really, really struggled to fit in.

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:19.380
I really struggled to
be able to come up with

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:22.623
the right words to get other
kids to want to talk to me.

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:27.970
And so I, I stood out pretty badly

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:31.410
and that continued throughout

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:33.660
my entire time in grade school.

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:37.320
I remember first feeling
what depression was

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:39.510
when I was around six.

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:40.890
- So when I was in university

00:03:40.890 --> 00:03:43.770
they make us do these
psychological studies

00:03:43.770 --> 00:03:45.480
'cause I was studying psychology

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:49.170
and they have different
things you can apply for

00:03:49.170 --> 00:03:51.810
and I noticed one of them was

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:54.180
they were studying autism in women,

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:56.400
specifically like people who are older.

00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:58.890
So I applied and that was
how I got my diagnosis

00:03:58.890 --> 00:04:02.280
which was like, I was 21 then.

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:04.140
My wife and like one of my friends

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:06.780
are actually the only people
that know I've been diagnosed.

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:10.110
So, surprise, family or
friends, anyone who's watching!

00:04:10.110 --> 00:04:11.250
It was like an eye opener.

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:13.680
It was kind of bittersweet
'cause it was like,

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:16.200
I'm happy now. I have
answers to all things

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:17.970
that were going on before
that I thought there was,

00:04:17.970 --> 00:04:20.070
might have been something wrong with me.

00:04:20.070 --> 00:04:23.880
- There is nothing wrong with
you, okay? You're amazing.

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:24.778
- I agree with that.

00:04:24.778 --> 00:04:26.556
- [Sophia] Thank you. I
agree with that. I, I agree.

00:04:26.556 --> 00:04:27.389
I agree now.

00:04:27.389 --> 00:04:28.222
- Chai, go ahead, yeah.

00:04:28.222 --> 00:04:30.930
- Yeah. I was diagnosed
with aut when I was a child

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:34.020
but I didn't know I was
autistic 'cause I mean

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:36.030
as a 2, 3, 4, year old, I mean,

00:04:36.030 --> 00:04:37.950
I don't know how one even knows

00:04:37.950 --> 00:04:39.510
what's supposed to be up with them.

00:04:39.510 --> 00:04:41.760
So it, it's definitely hard to like,

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:43.440
to speak in a group situation

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:46.590
'cause I can never tell
the rules of like how,

00:04:46.590 --> 00:04:48.570
like when to talk and when to speak up.

00:04:48.570 --> 00:04:50.010
It's kinda like just jumping in like

00:04:50.010 --> 00:04:53.220
right when you're kind of feeling like it.

00:04:53.220 --> 00:04:56.010
Plus I've also blocked a
lot of my childhood memories

00:04:56.010 --> 00:04:56.940
from trauma.

00:04:56.940 --> 00:04:58.636
- Trauma. (laughs)

00:04:58.636 --> 00:04:59.469
- And so I lived my whole life

00:04:59.469 --> 00:05:03.626
just wondering why I
couldn't connect to people.

00:05:03.626 --> 00:05:04.560
Understand each other.
- Understand feelings, yeah.

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:07.710
- Yeah. Can I ask you
guys a question about like

00:05:07.710 --> 00:05:10.110
what... what stimulation, stimulants like,

00:05:10.110 --> 00:05:11.220
do you guys like chew gum

00:05:11.220 --> 00:05:13.920
or do you guys like, have
anything that you guys

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:16.740
do specifically to
like, keep you guys calm

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:18.720
or keep you guys...

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:19.648
Go ahead Amanda.

00:05:19.648 --> 00:05:20.839
- The color pink!

00:05:20.839 --> 00:05:22.238
- The color pink. Okay.

00:05:22.238 --> 00:05:23.071
(group murmuring in agreement)

00:05:23.071 --> 00:05:24.020
- It's my favorite color
in the whole world.

00:05:24.020 --> 00:05:25.890
My whole room is pink at home.

00:05:25.890 --> 00:05:26.910
My bed is pink.

00:05:26.910 --> 00:05:27.960
My bed's back is pink.

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:29.940
My pillows are pink and my rug is pink

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:31.056
and my carpet is pink.

00:05:31.056 --> 00:05:32.700
And I even have a framed pink picture.

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:34.080
And most of my clothes are pink.

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:36.480
I love pink, it's the best
color in the whole world.

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:37.610
- Okay, okay. I've never heard someone...

00:05:37.610 --> 00:05:38.707
- [Darius] I love blue too.

00:05:38.707 --> 00:05:40.588
- I like blue too, but pink is better.

00:05:40.588 --> 00:05:41.790
- Oh, okay. (laughs)

00:05:41.790 --> 00:05:42.810
- I mean, it's subjective.

00:05:42.810 --> 00:05:45.210
- How do you guys feel about
the term high functioning?

00:05:45.210 --> 00:05:46.517
- [Amanda] I don't like that term at all.

00:05:46.517 --> 00:05:47.880
- I don't really think about it too much.

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:49.380
- I don't think I think about it either.

00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:50.711
That much either.

00:05:50.711 --> 00:05:52.050
- Autism is too much of a spectrum.

00:05:52.050 --> 00:05:53.310
So many people with autism have

00:05:53.310 --> 00:05:54.870
so many different experiences.

00:05:54.870 --> 00:05:57.390
- I can't choose any of you
guys to be the mole, like...

00:05:57.390 --> 00:05:58.986
- This is gonna be the hardest thing.

00:05:58.986 --> 00:06:00.090
- Even me, myself, like, it's like,

00:06:00.090 --> 00:06:02.040
I think I'm the mole, honestly.

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:03.390
- Oh my God. They were like,
"try to find the mole".

00:06:03.390 --> 00:06:04.223
And we're like, "maybe there's none".

00:06:04.223 --> 00:06:05.056
- There's no mole!

00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:08.550
- Even the person who's neurotypical

00:06:08.550 --> 00:06:10.448
might just be an undiagnosed autistic.

00:06:10.448 --> 00:06:13.380
(group laughing and
murmuring in agreement)

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:16.290
- Like damn, all this
relates, wait a second... huh.

00:06:16.290 --> 00:06:18.810
- The mole might not be neurotypical.

00:06:18.810 --> 00:06:21.120
Just because someone doesn't
have autism doesn't mean...

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:24.060
The mole could have like ADHD or OCD.

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:24.893
- That's true.

00:06:25.890 --> 00:06:29.010
- And could be overlapping
the symptoms that are,

00:06:29.010 --> 00:06:31.530
that could be associated
with autism as well.

00:06:31.530 --> 00:06:36.530
- My kid has a genetic
condition that is not autism

00:06:36.900 --> 00:06:40.440
but does manifest behaviorally
and neurologically

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:42.900
in many ways that are similar to autism.

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:46.470
So when, when people interact with my kid

00:06:46.470 --> 00:06:49.140
they often see an autistic child

00:06:49.140 --> 00:06:51.360
but my kid doesn't, isn't autistic.

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:54.000
- Does autism run in any
of your guys' family?

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:57.930
- I realize I was autistic
'cause my half-brother,

00:06:57.930 --> 00:07:00.480
he's like, he's five years old.

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:02.250
Well I guess he's eight, seven now.

00:07:02.250 --> 00:07:03.354
Whoops. I don't know his age.

00:07:03.354 --> 00:07:04.187
(group laughing)

00:07:04.187 --> 00:07:06.660
But like I, I see him, I'm like, "huh?

00:07:06.660 --> 00:07:07.980
Wasn't I like that when I was a kid?"

00:07:07.980 --> 00:07:09.330
My mom was like, "yeah, yeah,

00:07:09.330 --> 00:07:11.042
'cause we thought we thought
you were autistic too".

00:07:11.042 --> 00:07:11.875
And I was like, "what?"

00:07:11.875 --> 00:07:12.930
I had to found out that my,

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:15.240
that my youngest nephew
was just recently diagnosed

00:07:15.240 --> 00:07:16.350
with autism as well.

00:07:16.350 --> 00:07:19.410
So, but luckily he had an uncle
who had already experienced

00:07:19.410 --> 00:07:20.940
some of everything that
he's going to experience.

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:22.407
- That's awesome.

00:07:22.407 --> 00:07:24.450
- And I could be a mentor towards him.

00:07:24.450 --> 00:07:28.270
- Yeah. My dad is an undiagnosed
autistic. Sorry, Dad.

00:07:28.270 --> 00:07:29.400
(group laughing)

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:30.233
- Go go, go ahead.

00:07:30.233 --> 00:07:33.930
- Do you ever like wake up and
feel like you're not autistic

00:07:33.930 --> 00:07:36.090
and like, or like you have a disability,

00:07:36.090 --> 00:07:37.770
you're like, "this is just me", like...

00:07:37.770 --> 00:07:38.603
- Yes.

00:07:38.603 --> 00:07:39.436
- [Darius] No way.

00:07:39.436 --> 00:07:40.269
- It's called masking.

00:07:40.269 --> 00:07:44.670
- I think I could be seen as
neurotypical passing sometimes

00:07:44.670 --> 00:07:49.670
because I try to hide as
much symptoms as possible.

00:07:49.800 --> 00:07:52.830
'Cause I was trained to
do so at a very early age.

00:07:52.830 --> 00:07:54.990
- People that don't have experience

00:07:54.990 --> 00:07:56.640
with others who have autism,

00:07:56.640 --> 00:07:59.910
they expect us to stand out
and that's not always the case.

00:07:59.910 --> 00:08:03.210
You know, I mean we all,
we all look like humans.

00:08:03.210 --> 00:08:05.340
- I think you can blame that
partly on, you know, the media,

00:08:05.340 --> 00:08:06.480
how they portray us.

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:08.148
- We don't really have
a good representation.

00:08:08.148 --> 00:08:09.480
- [Kasian] No we don't.

00:08:09.480 --> 00:08:11.520
- What that movie with
Dustin Hoffman, you know?

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:12.480
Rain Man, you know?

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:14.280
Yeah. Where like, where, you know

00:08:14.280 --> 00:08:16.470
you having, you being on the spectrum,

00:08:16.470 --> 00:08:18.300
all it may mean, you're good at gambling.

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:20.550
I wish I was good at
gambling or something.

00:08:20.550 --> 00:08:22.800
- None of them have the
same experiences that I do.

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:24.697
So there's no way to have pinpointed,

00:08:24.697 --> 00:08:26.340
"oh this person is more quiet,

00:08:26.340 --> 00:08:28.020
this person is more socially awkward".

00:08:28.020 --> 00:08:30.720
There's no way to tell
who was autistic or not.

00:08:30.720 --> 00:08:32.400
- [Narrator] On the count of three,

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:35.763
will the mole please raise their hand?

00:08:36.810 --> 00:08:40.383
1, 2, 3.

00:08:41.393 --> 00:08:42.270
(group gasping)

00:08:42.270 --> 00:08:43.110
- Oh!

00:08:43.110 --> 00:08:44.520
- Who could have seen that coming?

00:08:44.520 --> 00:08:46.680
I totally didn't see that coming.

00:08:46.680 --> 00:08:47.822
- I was shook.

00:08:47.822 --> 00:08:50.340
- I was like, no it cannot be her.

00:08:50.340 --> 00:08:51.480
It can't, it can't be her.

00:08:51.480 --> 00:08:52.560
- I never would've guessed.

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:54.030
- I actually didn't lie about anything.

00:08:54.030 --> 00:08:56.970
I just flipped out autism with FASD

00:08:56.970 --> 00:09:00.120
and it stands for Fetal
Alcohol Spectrum Disorder.

00:09:00.120 --> 00:09:03.930
I was exposed to alcohol
when I was inside my mom

00:09:03.930 --> 00:09:06.570
in the womb for the first five months.

00:09:06.570 --> 00:09:11.250
And you can kind of think
of FASD as a spectrum,

00:09:11.250 --> 00:09:12.630
just like ASD.

00:09:12.630 --> 00:09:14.670
- Even though she might
not have been autistic,

00:09:14.670 --> 00:09:17.100
there are a lot of overlaps between autism

00:09:17.100 --> 00:09:19.110
and many other neurological disabilities.

00:09:19.110 --> 00:09:21.450
- FASD is never, never talked about.

00:09:21.450 --> 00:09:23.190
I've never met a single person

00:09:23.190 --> 00:09:26.670
who shares that diagnosis with me.

00:09:26.670 --> 00:09:28.350
- What differences do you see between

00:09:28.350 --> 00:09:30.510
people with autism versus?

00:09:30.510 --> 00:09:32.460
- I actually see a ton of similarities

00:09:32.460 --> 00:09:34.140
and I, I wouldn't wanna, you know

00:09:34.140 --> 00:09:36.030
discredit anybody's else's experience,

00:09:36.030 --> 00:09:38.400
but autism does run in
my family very heavily.

00:09:38.400 --> 00:09:39.750
My dad has autism.

00:09:39.750 --> 00:09:41.670
I have a sister that has autism

00:09:41.670 --> 00:09:44.220
and I have quite a few
cousins who have autism

00:09:44.220 --> 00:09:47.190
and I also have quite a few
friends who have autism.

00:09:47.190 --> 00:09:48.875
- Damn, we're not your first.

00:09:48.875 --> 00:09:50.820
(group laughing)

00:09:50.820 --> 00:09:52.950
- The autism community accepts you.

00:09:52.950 --> 00:09:53.970
- I love you guys.

00:09:53.970 --> 00:09:58.830
- Getting a diagnosis can
be a whirlwind of emotions.

00:09:58.830 --> 00:10:00.300
As long as you stay true to yourself

00:10:00.300 --> 00:10:02.970
I promise you everything's
gonna end up okay.

00:10:02.970 --> 00:10:05.940
- We are more than the
label that defines us.

00:10:05.940 --> 00:10:08.400
At each leap, what we
have is a whole spectrum.

00:10:08.400 --> 00:10:11.790
- A diagnosis does not define somebody.

00:10:11.790 --> 00:10:13.350
Everybody is completely unique.

00:10:13.350 --> 00:10:15.720
Whether you're neurotypical
or neurodivergent.

00:10:15.720 --> 00:10:17.637
- Thank you so much.
